1 MACRO

   Assembles VAX MACRO-32 source files into object modules (.OBJ), the
   same kind of file VMS's own MACRO writes for its linker.

   Format

     MACRO filespec[{,|+}...]

2 Parameter

 filespec[{,|+}...]

   The source file, or a list of them.  Files separated by commas are
   each assembled by themselves, to an object (and listing) of their
   own, and an error in one doesn't stop the next.  Files separated by
   plus signs are concatenated into one input, for a single object (and
   listing), named for the first file.  All have the same qualifiers,
   and /OBJECT= or /LIST= with a file name gets each assembly's output
   in turn.  Without a file type a source is .MAR.

   A source can be a host file or a file on a mounted volume:

     - with /HOST after it, it is a host path;
     - a name with a directory in brackets ([DIR]HELLO.MAR), or a
       device or logical name of two or more characters (DUA1:HELLO.MAR,
       SRC:HELLO.MAR), is on a mounted volume;
     - a name with "/" or "\" (or a Windows drive letter) is a host
       path;
     - any other name (HELLO.MAR) is in the default directory when SET
       DEFAULT names a mounted volume, and a host file otherwise.

   Quote a host path to keep its case; unquoted names are made
   uppercase, as all DCL input is.  LINK, LIBRARY, ANALYZE, FORTRAN, and
   RUN find their files by the same rules (see SPECIFY).

2 Description

   Every error in the source is reported, and then no object module is
   written, so a failed assembly never replaces a good object.  Warnings
   are reported, and the object is still written.  .INCLUDE and .LIBRARY
   file names follow the same rules as the source's, and a bare name is
   found beside the source.

   A macro the source calls but doesn't define (a system macro such as
   $EXIT_S or $QIOW_S) is looked for in the macro libraries, in VMS
   MACRO's order: the .LIBRARY names, the last named first; then the
   /LIBRARY names, the last named first; then STARLET.MLB.  STARLET.MLB
   is SYS$LIBRARY:STARLET.MLB on a mounted volume, or else STARLET.MLB in
   the host directory the vax.library setting names, or else govax's
   own.  govax's own has the RMS macros ($FAB, $RAB, $NAM, the XABs,
   their _STORE forms, and $OPEN, $GET, $PUT, and the other services),
   the symbol definition macros ($FABDEF, $RMSDEF, $SSDEF, $IODEF,
   $SECDEF, $LCKDEF, $PSLDEF, $DCDEF, and others), and the system
   service macros ($EXIT_S, $QIOW_S, $CREPRC_S, $CRMPSC_S, $ENQ_S,
   $FAO_S, $TRNLNM_S, $GETSYI_S, and the rest), each also as NAME (an
   argument list in line) and NAME_G (CALLG), giving the same code as VAX
   MACRO's.  Macro libraries are made with LIBRARY/MACRO.

   In the source, .SHOW and .NOSHOW (or .LIST and .NLIST) with options
   turn listing options on and off; without options they raise and
   lower the listing level, and nothing is listed below level 0.  .PAGE
   starts a new page, and .SBTTL names the pages that follow and adds a
   line to the table of contents, page 0.  .NOCROSS leaves the lines that
   follow out of the cross reference, until a .CROSS; with symbols
   named, it leaves out just those symbols, until a .CROSS names them.

   From the host shell, "govax macro filespec..." runs this command, with
   --object file or --no-object, --list or --list-file file, --show and
   --no-show option,..., and --library library (which may be repeated).
   --mount DEVICE=container (or --mount-write, to allow writing) mounts a
   container first, and may be repeated.  govax exits with a nonzero
   status if the command fails.

2 Qualifiers
/CROSS_REFERENCE

      /[NO]CROSS_REFERENCE[=(option[,...])]

   Ends the listing (with /LIST) with a cross reference: for each name,
   the line that defined it and the lines that referred to it.  The
   options say what it lists: SYMBOLS, MACROS, OPCODES (instructions),
   DIRECTIVES, REGISTERS, ALL, or NONE.  Without options it lists
   symbols and macros.  A reference marked #- is an instruction's
   register operand, or a symbol in a literal operand (#VALUE).  The
   default is /NOCROSS_REFERENCE.

/DEBUG

      /[NO]DEBUG[=(option[,...])]

   What the object carries for the debugger and for traceback, the
   symbolic stack dump VMS prints when a program fails.  The options are
   TRACEBACK (traceback records), SYMBOLS (debugger records, and no
   traceback), ALL (both; the same as /DEBUG alone), and NONE.  /NODEBUG
   is /DEBUG=NONE, which writes no traceback records either.  The
   default is /DEBUG=TRACEBACK.  Debugger records give the source file,
   a line-number table of the code, and, with traceback too, a record of
   each symbol and its data type.  As with VMS MACRO, .ENABLE DEBUG part
   way through a source starts them there, and .DISABLE DEBUG doesn't
   stop them.

/DISABLE

      /DISABLE=(function[,...])

   Turns assembler functions off at the start of the source, as
   .DISABLE does in it.  The functions are /ENABLE's.  /DISABLE=TRACEBACK
   writes no traceback records.

/ENABLE

      /ENABLE=(function[,...])

   Turns assembler functions on at the start of the source, as .ENABLE
   does in it: ABSOLUTE (AMA), DEBUG (DBG), GLOBAL (GBL), SUPPRESSION
   (SUP), TRACEBACK (TBK), TRUNCATION (FPT), and VECTOR.  The source's
   own .ENABLE and .DISABLE can still change them.  /DEBUG is applied
   first, then /ENABLE and /DISABLE.

/HOST

   After a source, makes it a host path, whatever it looks like.

/LIBRARY

      /LIBRARY=(library[,...])

   Macro libraries (.MLB, the default type) to search for macros the
   source calls but doesn't define, ahead of STARLET.MLB.  A bare name
   is found beside the source.  This takes the place of VMS's "MACRO
   PROG+LIB/LIBRARY".

/LIST

      /[NO]LIST[=filespec]

   Writes a listing file: the source beside the bytes each line
   produced, in 60-line pages with headings, then the symbol table, the
   psect synopsis, the run statistics, and the command line, laid out as
   VAX MACRO lays out its listings.  The listing is named and placed as
   the object is, with the type LIS.  It is written even when the
   assembly finds errors.  The heading shows the source's full file
   specification and when it was last revised.  The default is /NOLIST.

/OBJECT

      /[NO]OBJECT[=filespec]

   Writes the object module (the default).  Without a file name, the
   object has the source's name and location, with the type OBJ in the
   same case as the source's type: hello.mar gives hello.obj, HELLO.MAR
   gives HELLO.OBJ.  A bare file name also goes beside the source; a host
   path or a VMS file specification goes where it says.  On a volume,
   the object is the next version of its name, and the volume must be
   mounted /WRITE.  /NOOBJECT assembles the source and reports any
   errors, but writes no object.

/SHOW

      /[NO]SHOW=(option[,...])

   Turns listing options on (/SHOW) or off (/NOSHOW) for the whole
   listing, whatever the source's .SHOW and .NOSHOW say: BINARY (MEB),
   the lines of a macro expansion that store bytes; CALLS (MC), macro
   calls; CONDITIONALS (CND), conditional directives and the lines they
   leave out; DEFINITIONS (MD), macro definitions; and EXPANSIONS (ME),
   every line of a macro expansion.  By default, all but BINARY and
   EXPANSIONS are on.

2 Examples

   1.$ MACRO "hello.mar"

     Assembles the host file hello.mar into hello.obj.

   2.$ MACRO DUA1:[SRC]HELLO /OBJECT=DUA1:[OBJ]

     Assembles HELLO.MAR into DUA1:[OBJ]HELLO.OBJ.

   3.$ MACRO "hello.mar"/HOST /LIST /OBJECT=DUA1:[000000]HELLO.OBJ

     Assembles a host file into a volume's object, with a listing.

   4.$ MACRO PROG /LIBRARY=(MYMACROS,COMMON.MLB)

     Assembles PROG.MAR, searching two macro libraries.

   5.% govax --mount-write DUA1=work.dsk macro DUA1:[000000]HELLO.MAR

     Assembles a volume's file from the host shell.
